---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="RESO containers"
  description="The main container format the game uses. A 16-byte header, a table of contents, then the data chunks."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'RESO' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    RESO is the container format that holds almost everything in the game.
    Out of the 2,441 asset files in the ROM, <strong>2,343 of them are
    RESO containers</strong> (or compressed wrappers around RESO containers).
    The remaining 98 are raw files of one kind or another.
  </p>

  <h2>The header</h2>
  <p>
    A RESO file always starts with a 16-byte header:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`+0x00  4 bytes   "RESO"        magic
+0x04  4 bytes   file_count    number of entries in the TOC
+0x08  4 bytes   version       "1.21" / "1.11" / "1.10" / "1.01"
+0x0c  4 bytes   variant       0 or 1 — picks the TOC entry size`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Of 2,343 containers in the game, version <code>1.21</code> covers
    2,148 of them. The other three versions are rare exceptions.
  </p>

  <h2>The table of contents</h2>
  <p>
    Right after the header is a list of entries — the table of contents.
    Each entry says how big a chunk is and where it sits in the file.
    There are two layouts, picked by the <code>variant</code> field:
  </p>

  <p><strong>Variant 0 (16 bytes per entry):</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{`+0x00  u32      unknown (often 0)
+0x04  4 bytes  type tag (4 ASCII chars, stored reversed)
+0x08  u32      length of the chunk
+0x0c  u32      offset where the chunk starts (absolute)`}</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Variant 1 (8 bytes per entry, no per-entry tag):</strong></p>
  <pre><code>{`+0x00  u32  length
+0x04  u32  offset`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Variant 1 shows up on the message dialog files — there's no need for
    a per-entry tag because every entry is the same kind (a dialog payload).
  </p>

  <h2>The type tags</h2>
  <p>
    In variant-0 containers, every entry carries a 4-character type tag
    saying what kind of data it is. Like everything else in the file
    format, the bytes are reversed — read right to left to get the
    "real" name.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tag in file</th><th>Reversed</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>rgcn</code></td><td>NCGR</td><td>Tile sheet graphics</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rlcn</code></td><td>NCLR</td><td>Color palette</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rcsn</code></td><td>NSCR</td><td>Background tilemap</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recn</code></td><td>NCER</td><td>Sprite definition (OAM cells)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rnan</code></td><td>NANR</td><td>Sprite animation</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>rtfn</code></td><td>NFTR</td><td>Font</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ncbr</code></td><td>NCBR</td><td>Linear/bitmap variant of NCGR</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tbsn</code></td><td>NSBTX</td><td>3D texture pack</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mbsn</code></td><td>NSBMD</td><td>3D model</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cbsn</code></td><td>NSBCA</td><td>3D animation</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>szl</code></td><td>—</td><td>Nested compressed sub-blob (decompress for another RESO)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tad</code></td><td>—</td><td>Konami's static widget table format</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>nib</code></td><td>"bin"</td><td>Konami's per-container binary data, variable layout</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sfo</code></td><td>—</td><td>A nested RESO container</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    The first ten are standard Nintendo Nitro formats — see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/nitro-graphics/`}>Nitro
    graphics</a>. The last four are Konami's own extensions — see
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/konami-extensions/`}>Konami
    extensions</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Containers can hold other containers</h2>
  <p>
    A RESO entry can itself be another RESO container, or a compressed
    blob that decompresses to one. The title screen pack
    (<code>2d/mainmenu/title_pack.ofs</code>) does exactly this: the
    outer container has five entries, each of which is an LZ11-compressed
    inner RESO container with the actual tile sheet, palette, and tilemap
    for one seasonal variant of the title.
  </p>

  <h2>What's in most files</h2>
  <p>
    Most containers are small — under a dozen entries. The big ones are
    typically:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Message dialog files</strong> (1,328 files): always
      exactly 100 slots, of which only some are populated. The empty
      slots are kept as zero-length placeholders so dialog can be
      addressed by a fixed index.</li>
    <li><strong>Item icons</strong> (<code>item/itemicon.ofs</code>):
      656 tile-sheet entries in a single container, no palette inside
      (it lives in <code>global.lzs</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Magic glyphs</strong>
      (<code>2d/inputmagic/msg.ofs</code>): 1,000 slots of which 377
      are populated, holding the JP labels for the magic-language
      keyboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Cooking recipes</strong>
      (<code>ucc/names.ofs</code>): 192 slots holding food and recipe
      names. Some slots are arrays of sub-records keyed by ingredient
      pair — see <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/food-recipes/`}>food
      recipes</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Rebuilding a container</h2>
  <p>
    When we translate a dialog file, we have to write a new RESO container
    back out. Two rules matter:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Every chunk's offset has to be 16-byte aligned, so we pad
      between chunks with zero bytes if needed.</li>
    <li>The TOC must list every original entry, even the empty ones. The
      engine looks up dialog by fixed index, so a missing slot 47 would
      misalign everything past it.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Empty slots are written with length 0 and offset 0. That's the same
    thing the original file does.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
